  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 353 AM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis places deep low along the border of\nnortheast Wyoming and southeast Montana, with high over the\nwestern Great Lakes. Upper level analysis places low along and\njust north of the international border between southern Alberta\nand northwest Montana. Over our area, a variety of short waves\nlifting in southwest flow ahead of the low area continue to\nproduce showers and some thunderstorms now spreading through\nwestern and central North Dakota, while gusty southerly to \nsoutheasterly winds continue to the northeast of the \naforementioned surface low.\n\nFor today, upper low will continue to track easterly along the\ninternational border with rain showers continuing to fill in\nearly this morning. In the meantime, surface low transitions\nnorthward, crossing the intersection of the Montana, North\nDakota, and Saskatchewan borders around mid-day. As it lifts\nnorthward this morning, drier air works in behind as a frontal\nboundary pushes in, with precipitation gradually ending from \nsouthwest to northeast. Ahead of the wind switch, breezy\nsoutherly winds will continue, but behind the boundary, winds\nwill become westerly during the morning over western North \nDakota, and the afternoon central. Strongest winds will be over \nwestern North Dakota where ample mixing of stronger winds aloft \nshown on latest forecast soundings should result in gusts to 60 \nmph this afternoon into this evening. The argument for stronger\nwinds also continues to be reflected in the latest ECMWF-EFI \nwhich has been persistant with values of 0.9 and above in this \narea, and even showing some 0.95 with the latest run, readings \nwhich have historically been in line with high wind warning \nwinds developing. The fly in the ointment, though, may be the \nrather modest at best cold air advection, but still think the \nstrong winds will mix down. Therefore, have decided to upgrade \nto a High Wind Warning generally from Hettinger to Dickinson to \nMinot and points westward. Further east, wind gusts of 50 to 55 \nmph are expected, but will continue into the overnight hours \nbefore starting to back off. Have decided to add McIntosh and \nDickey Counties to the Advisory given the anticipated higher \nwinds spreading further southeast than previously thought. \n\nA concern that develops with the high winds and the drier air\nmoving in from southwest to northeast is fire weather,\nespecially over the southwest where humidity values may dip into\nthe 30s, possibly some upper 20s over the far southwest, which\nmay dry out fuels depending on how much precipitation falls. For\nnow, will advertise near critical fire weather conditions, but\nwill have to keep a close eye on our far southwest to see if a\nRed Flag Warning may be needed if only little precipitation\nfalls. \n\nSystem pushes off to the east-northeast for Tuesday, with\nsurface low sliding through Ontario. Some breezy conditions may\nremain over central North Dakota in the morning, but should be\ntapering off through he afternoon as the low continues its \ndeparture. Dry conditions expected in its wake. \n\nOn Wednesday a rather strong by fast moving short wave moves\nthrough, bringing a chance for rain showers to the area. Pattern\nremains unsettled with low to medium precipitation chances in \nthe forecast to close out the week. Modest warming trend \nexpected for the last part of the work week.",
